.claude/

# Backend local secrets (never commit)
SECRETS.local.txt
**/SECRETS.local.txt

# Visual Studio / Rider IDE state (machine-specific)
**/.vs/
**/.idea/
*.suo
*.user
*.lscache
*.dtbcache.v2
*.futdcache.v2
*.vsidx

# Local developer overrides — each machine keeps its own copy, never committed
**/appsettings.Local.json
**/appsettings.*.Local.json

# Publish profiles carry deployment paths and connection strings
**/PublishProfiles/*.user
**/serviceDependencies.local.json.user

# Pillar 2 — lease signing key (supply via secret store / copy out-of-band)
**/lease_signing_key.pem

# Ignore build output and intermediate files
**/obj/
**/bin/

# macOS / Xcode
**/.DS_Store
**/__MACOSX/
**/xcuserdata/
**/*.xcuserstate
**/DerivedData/

# The redirected build output. `dotnet build` cannot write to the API's normal
# bin/ while the app is running under the VS debugger (MSB3021, the exe is
# locked), so builds are pointed at /build via BaseOutputPath. It is ~300MB of
# artifacts and it carries COPIES of appsettings.Local.json - ignored by name
# above, but there is no reason for any of it to be near the index.
#
# NOT anchored to the repo root. The leading-slash version only matched /build/,
# so output that landed at Back-End/Web-POS.Api/build/ sailed straight past it
# and 736 DLLs were committed. Gradle writes Front-End/android/build/ too.
**/build/

# Directories named after a MANGLED COMMAND LINE. A mis-parsed
# `dotnet build -o ... --nologo` created real folders called `build/logo` and
# `POS-APPbuildtest --nologoDebug`, each holding a full copy of the build
# output, and they were committed because nothing matched them. The `--nologo`
# half is caught by the rule above; this catches the rest.
**/*--nologo*/
**/POS-APPbuild*/

# Agent skills materialized by `npx skills add` (higgsfield-ai/skills et al).
# Treated like node_modules: skills-lock.json is committed so the set is
# reproducible, the unpacked skill files are not.
/.agents/

# ── SSH and server private keys — NEVER commit. THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. ──
# server-pos.ppk turned up in the repo root during the production cutover, and
# Step 1 of RELEASE_RUNBOOK.md is literally `git add .` — one routine commit
# would have published the production server's SSH key to the whole internet.
# No .pem or .ppk has ever been tracked here, so these patterns cost nothing.
*.ppk
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa
id_ed25519

# Source art for the site. The exported WebP in website/public/ is what ships;
# these are the heavy originals from the generator (a 1.4MB PNG hero, etc.) and
# there is no reason for them near the index — see audit H2.
/website/assets-src/

